.TH VTIUM.CONF 5 2026-11-17 "vtium 0.1.0-alpha.9" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
vtium.conf \- the configuration file of vtium, a terminal for the Linux console
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/vtium/vtium.conf\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
vtium reads \fB/etc/vtium/vtium.conf\fR when it starts on a virtual terminal (VT). The file sets vtium's settings, such as its font, its colors and its key bindings. Its sections say which displays and input devices belong to which screen.
.PP
vtium reads the file once, when it starts. A change takes effect when vtium starts again, for example when its service restarts. vtium never changes the file.
.PP
vtium reads the file only when root owns it. For a vtium that runs as another user, that user may own it instead. Neither the file's group nor other users may be able to write to the file, or to a directory above it. vtium reads none of a file that fails this check, and runs its built-in configuration instead.
.PP
Without the file, vtium uses the defaults below and its built-in sections. \fB/etc/vtium/vtium.conf.example\fR lists every setting at its default, commented out.
.PP
\fBvtium check\-config FILE\fR checks a file before you install it. \fBvtium\-config\fR edits a copy of the file and installs the copy. See vtium(8) and vtium-config(1).
.SH FORMAT
The file has two parts. Every line above the first section heading is the top level. The top level holds vtium's settings, which every screen uses unless its own section sets them, and the two keys of the sections, \fBversion\fR and \fBpolicy\fR. The sections start at the first heading in brackets, such as \fB[look big]\fR.
.PP
A top-level line holds one setting, written \fBkey=value\fR, or for a switch, \fBkey\fR to turn it on and \fBno\-key\fR to turn it off. A section's line is \fBkey = value\fR. vtium drops the spaces and tabs around the key and the value.
.PP
A \fB#\fR starts a comment at the start of a line, or after a space or a tab. A \fB#\fR inside a word is part of the value, and so is a \fB#\fR that opens a \fB#rrggbb\fR color. \fB\e#\fR after a space is a literal \fB#\fR.
.PP
vtium reports a top-level key that it does not know, and a value that it cannot use. vtium clamps a number that is out of range, and ignores a value that it cannot read, keeping the default. An error in a section never drops a top-level setting. vtium reads at most 65536 bytes of the file, and 8192 bytes of a line.
.PP
Each top-level setting is also an option of vtium's command line, which wins over the file. On the command line, a setting is \fB\-\-key=VALUE\fR or \fB\-\-key VALUE\fR, and a switch is \fB\-\-key\fR or \fB\-\-no\-key\fR.
.SH SETTINGS
.SS "Fonts and text"
The console's font and its size, who draws box-drawing and block characters, how blinking text looks, and which way text runs.
.TP
\fBfont-name\fR
The console's font.
.IP
The value is the name of a font set (a frit file, \fBNAME.vtfrit\fR in \fB/etc/vtium/fonts\fR). It can also be the name of a kbd console font, or with \fBfont\-engine = psf\fR the path of a PSF font; vtium then uses that one font.
.IP
When unset, vtium uses the font from vconsole.conf. If there is none, or vtium cannot find the font, vtium uses its built-in font and logs why.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-font\-name=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBfont-frits\fR
The directory that holds the font sets (the \fBNAME.vtfrit\fR files that \fBfont\-name\fR names).
.IP
It must be an absolute path; vtium ignores a relative or empty one and logs why.
.IP
The default is the directory vtium was built with.
.IP
It takes an absolute path: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: \fB/etc/vtium/fonts\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-font\-frits=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBfont-paths\fR
The folders where vtium looks, in order, for installed fonts that a font set's \fBfont:\fR lines name.
.IP
Separate the folders with \fB:\fR, as in \fBPATH\fR. Each folder must be an absolute path. The list replaces the built-in list. vtium skips a folder that is not an absolute path, logs why, and uses the rest.
.IP
The list can reorder or leave out the system's folders.
.IP
When unset, vtium searches \fB/usr/share/fonts\fR and \fB/usr/local/share/fonts\fR. vtium searches a user's own folder, such as \fB~/.local/share/fonts\fR, only when the list names it. The font helper cannot read such a folder before someone logs in, because the helper then runs as \fBnobody\fR.
.IP
It takes absolute paths, separated by \fB:\fR: at most 4095 bytes in all, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-font\-paths=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBfont-engine\fR
Which kind of font to use. When unset, vtium tries each kind the build supports, in the order listed.
.IP
It takes one of \fBfreetype\fR, \fBpango\fR, \fBpsf\fR, \fBunifont\fR, \fB8x16\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-font\-engine=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBfont-ligatures\fR
Draw a programming font's ligatures, such as \fB\->\fR and \fB!=\fR, across the character cells of their characters.
.IP
Each character keeps its own cell. A ligature splits into its separate characters while the cursor is on one of them, while a selection covers only part of it, or when its characters differ in color or style. Bitmap fonts and the built-in fonts show no ligatures.
.IP
It takes one of \fBoff\fR, \fBon\fR. Default: \fBoff\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-font\-ligatures=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBfont-size\fR
The font size in points (1/72 inch).
.IP
The size in pixels is points \[u00D7] DPI \[u00F7] 72, at the display's density (\fBdisplay\-dpi\fR). It overrides a font set's \fBcell=\fR line. 0 picks a size to suit each display.
.IP
It takes a number in pt: 0 to 2048; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 0.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-font\-size=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBcell\fR
The size of a look's character cell, written as in a font set's \fBcell=\fR line (WIDTHxHEIGHT).
.IP
It overrides the \fBcell=\fR and \fBsize=\fR lines of the look's font set, and the top-level \fBfont\-size\fR. A \fBfont\-size\fR other than 0 in the same look overrides \fBcell\fR. When unset, the font set's \fBcell=\fR decides.
.IP
It takes a character cell in pixels, \fBWIDTHxHEIGHT\fR: each side 6 to 96, with no leading zero. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in a \fB[look NAME]\fR section.
.TP
\fBfont-hinting\fR
How outline fonts are fitted to the pixel grid (hinting). It overrides a font set's \fBhinting=\fR line.
.IP
\fBlight\fR fits each character vertically only. \fBfull\fR fits a TrueType font both ways, and treats a CFF font as \fBlight\fR. Bitmap fonts are not hinted.
.IP
This key has no effect yet: each font set's \fBhinting=\fR line decides, and \fBlight\fR is used where it has none.
.IP
It takes one of \fBlight\fR, \fBfull\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-font\-hinting=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBfont-line-spacing\fR
Extra space between lines of text. It overrides a font set's \fBline\-spacing=\fR line.
.IP
The value is a whole percentage of the text height, rounded to whole pixels and split above and below each line, so the console has fewer lines. When \fBfont\-size\fR overrides a font set's \fBcell=\fR size, the character cell grows by the spacing. When unset, the font set's \fBline\-spacing=\fR decides, and 0 where it has none.
.IP
Letters keep their size in the middle of the taller line, and line and block characters stretch to fill it. A font set's \fBfit=\fR lines choose this for each set of characters.
.IP
It takes a number in %: 0 to 100; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-font\-line\-spacing=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBglyph-source\fR
Who draws box-drawing, block and similar characters (six ranges): the font, or vtium itself.
.IP
\fBfont\fR uses the font's characters, \fBsprites\fR uses vtium's own drawings, and \fBauto\fR uses the font's where the font has the character. When unset, vtium uses the font for each range the font covers completely and its own drawings for the rest; with the built-in unifont, vtium always draws them itself.
.IP
It takes one of \fBfont\fR, \fBsprites\fR, \fBauto\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-glyph\-source=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBglyph-source-box\fR
Overrides \fBglyph\-source\fR for box drawing (U+2500 to U+257F).
.IP
It takes one of \fBfont\fR, \fBsprites\fR, \fBauto\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-glyph\-source\-box=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBglyph-source-block\fR
Overrides \fBglyph\-source\fR for block elements (U+2580 to U+259F).
.IP
It takes one of \fBfont\fR, \fBsprites\fR, \fBauto\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-glyph\-source\-block=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBglyph-source-braille\fR
Overrides \fBglyph\-source\fR for Braille (U+2800 to U+28FF).
.IP
It takes one of \fBfont\fR, \fBsprites\fR, \fBauto\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-glyph\-source\-braille=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBglyph-source-sextants\fR
Overrides \fBglyph\-source\fR for sextants (U+1FB00 to U+1FB3B).
.IP
It takes one of \fBfont\fR, \fBsprites\fR, \fBauto\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-glyph\-source\-sextants=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBglyph-source-octants\fR
Overrides \fBglyph\-source\fR for octants (U+1CD00 to U+1CDE5).
.IP
It takes one of \fBfont\fR, \fBsprites\fR, \fBauto\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-glyph\-source\-octants=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBglyph-source-legacy\fR
Overrides \fBglyph\-source\fR for the other block shapes of Symbols for Legacy Computing.
.IP
It takes one of \fBfont\fR, \fBsprites\fR, \fBauto\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-glyph\-source\-legacy=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBmissing\fR
What vtium shows for a character the font cannot display: \fBbox\fR shows \[u25A1] (U+25A1), and \fBhex\fR shows the character's code in hex digits.
.IP
It takes one of \fBbox\fR, \fBhex\fR. Default: \fBbox\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-missing=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBblink\fR
Blink blinking text (SGR 5) every 500 ms, and start each session with a blinking block cursor. When off, the text stays steady and the cursor starts as a steady block.
.IP
A program can choose another cursor style with DECSCUSR, and that style blinks or stays steady as it says. A program can also set another pace for the cursor with the Linux console's \fBCSI 16 ; n ]\fR.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-blink\fR and \fB\-\-no\-blink\fR.
.TP
\fBblink-text\fR
How blinking text (SGR 5 or 6) looks while a blink is off.
.IP
\fBfull\fR hides it, \fBpartial\fR dims it halfway to its background, as faint text (SGR 2) is, and \fBoff\fR keeps it steady. The cursor follows \fBblink\fR either way.
.IP
It takes one of \fBfull\fR, \fBpartial\fR, \fBoff\fR. Default: \fBpartial\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-blink\-text=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBblink-decorations\fR
Blink the underline and strikethrough of blinking text with it.
.IP
They hide while a blink is off with \fBblink\-text = full\fR, and dim with \fBpartial\fR. When off, they stay visible while the text blinks.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: off.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-blink\-decorations\fR and \fB\-\-no\-blink\-decorations\fR.
.TP
\fBallow-font-ops\fR
Let programs switch the console's font, and ask which font is in use, with OSC 50.
.IP
A program switches with \fBOSC 50 ; NAME ST\fR and asks with \fBOSC 50 ; ? ST\fR. vtium reads both as xterm's control sequences define OSC 50.
.IP
A switch lasts until that program's session ends. vtium then returns to the font and size that \fBfont\-name\fR and \fBfont\-size\fR set.
.IP
\fBvtium\-config use\-frit NAME\fR switches the font with the same sequence.
.IP
The query reports the name of the font on screen. A font file's path or a font family is not a name a switch takes, so the query then reports no name.
.IP
When off, vtium ignores OSC 50, queries included.
.IP
It is on by default. A program can choose only an installed font set, a built-in font or the font its console started in, never another file.
.IP
vtium reads a font set only when root owns it and no other user can change it or a folder above it.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-allow\-font\-ops\fR and \fB\-\-no\-allow\-font\-ops\fR.
.TP
\fBallow-window-ops\fR
Let programs set the size of the console's text area in rows and columns (xterm's window operation \fBCSI 8 ; ROWS ; COLUMNS t\fR).
.IP
vtium meets the request by changing the font size. It draws at the largest font size at which the displays hold that many rows and columns, and the console's grid becomes exactly that size. When the displays cannot hold the size even at the smallest font size, the grid gets as many rows and columns as they hold there. A number left out keeps that side as it is, and 0 fills the display on that side.
.IP
The grid is exact, but a display may draw it in larger character cells than that font size gives, to fill more of the screen. A program that asks for the cell size, as with \fBCSI 16 t\fR, is told the cell of that font size.
.IP
The size lasts until the session of the program that asked for it ends, or until you zoom. Then vtium returns to the size that \fBfont\-size\fR sets.
.IP
When off, vtium ignores the request and changes nothing. It is off by default, as xterm's \fBallowWindowOps\fR is. A program can ask for the size in any case, with \fBCSI 18 t\fR.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: off.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-allow\-window\-ops\fR and \fB\-\-no\-allow\-window\-ops\fR.
.TP
\fBallow-display-ops\fR
Let a program in a console hand the display to a graphical program, such as a video player or a kiosk application, and take it back, without a VT switch.
.IP
The program sends \fBOSC setBackground ST\fR to hand the display over, and \fBOSC setForeground ST\fR to take it back. These are kmscon's private sequences. Only the console that a head shows may hand the display over. A head is the terminal that vtium runs on one or more displays.
.IP
vtium then gives up each graphics card that drives a display of that head, so the graphical program can open the card and draw. Other heads with displays on those cards pause on those displays. vtium sends no keys or pointer events to a head whose every display has paused. The VT switch keys keep working.
.IP
vtium takes the display back at setForeground from the same console, when that console's session ends, when you switch away from vtium's VT and back, or when vtium restarts.
.IP
When off, vtium ignores both sequences. It is on by default.
.IP
When a seat manager, logind or seatd, gives vtium its seat, vtium ignores setBackground and logs that it did. A graphical program then runs in a session of its own on another VT.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-allow\-display\-ops\fR and \fB\-\-no\-allow\-display\-ops\fR.
.TP
\fBtext-direction\fR
Which way a paragraph of text runs when the program that writes it does not say.
.IP
A paragraph is the text between two line ends. \fBltr\fR, the default, runs each paragraph left to right, from the left edge. \fBrtl\fR runs it right to left, from the right edge. \fBauto\fR takes each paragraph's direction from its first letter. A paragraph that starts with an Arabic or Hebrew letter runs right to left, and any other paragraph runs left to right.
.IP
A paragraph keeps its direction however many rows it wraps across. \fBrtl\fR suits a console used mostly in Arabic, Hebrew or another right-to-left language.
.IP
vtium reads this key when it starts, so a change takes effect when vtium restarts. The key never changes text that is already on the screen.
.IP
A program can change the direction with escape sequences, and a terminal reset returns it to this setting.
.IP
A build without right-to-left support (the \fBbidi\fR feature) accepts this key and does nothing with it.
.IP
It takes one of \fBltr\fR, \fBrtl\fR, \fBauto\fR. Default: \fBltr\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-text\-direction=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBcluster-width\fR
How many character cells a syllable takes when it has a spacing vowel sign or a virama that joins two consonants, in Tamil, Devanagari and other scripts of South and Southeast Asia.
.IP
\fBdouble\fR, the default, gives such a syllable two cells, so \[u0B95]\[u0BCA] takes two. \fBsingle\fR gives it one cell and squeezes it to fit.
.IP
For example, the Tamil syllable \[u0B95]\[u0BCA] is the letter \[u0B95] with the vowel sign \[u0BCA]. Under \fBdouble\fR it is drawn at full size, and the shell's line editor counts it as two cells too. \fBsingle\fR suits programs that count one cell for each syllable, such as Neovim.
.IP
The choice comes from each character's Unicode properties, never from the font. A syllable takes two cells when a spacing vowel sign (General_Category Mc) follows its first letter, or when a virama joins a consonant to it, as Python wcwidth's specification counts them. No syllable takes more than two cells. A program that resets mode 2027 gives each character its own cells under either value.
.IP
vtium reads this key when it starts, so a change takes effect when vtium restarts.
.IP
It takes one of \fBdouble\fR, \fBsingle\fR. Default: \fBdouble\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-cluster\-width=VALUE\fR.
.SS Colors
The sixteen basic colors, the default text and background colors, and the colors of underlines and strikethrough.
.TP
\fBpalette\fR
The color scheme: the 16 basic colors, the default text and background colors, and the cursor and selection colors.
.IP
The value names a built-in palette, \fBcustom\fR, which takes the colors from the \fBpalette\-*\fR keys, or a file \fBNAME.palette\fR in \fB/etc/vtium/palettes\fR or \fB/usr/share/vtium/palettes\fR. A look's own \fBpalette\fR wins.
.IP
A palette file uses Ghostty's theme format. When a palette file is missing or has an error, vtium logs one line and draws the color scheme below it: the top level's in place of a look's, and \fBlegacy\fR in place of the top level's. See Color schemes <https://vtium.github.io/vtium/palettes/>.
.IP
It takes a name: one of \fBlegacy\fR, \fBcustom\fR, \fBnord\fR, \fBsolarized\fR, \fBsolarized\-black\fR, \fBsolarized\-white\fR, \fBsoft\-black\fR, \fBbase16\-dark\fR, \fBbase16\-light\fR, \fBvga\fR, or a file's name: 1 to 64 of the characters A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ + \-. Default: \fBlegacy\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-black\fR
Color 0 (black) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB0,0,0\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-black=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-red\fR
Color 1 (red) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB205,0,0\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-red=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-green\fR
Color 2 (green) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB0,205,0\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-green=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-yellow\fR
Color 3 (yellow) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB205,205,0\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-yellow=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-blue\fR
Color 4 (blue) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB0,0,238\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-blue=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-magenta\fR
Color 5 (magenta) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB205,0,205\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-magenta=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-cyan\fR
Color 6 (cyan) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB0,205,205\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-cyan=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-light-grey\fR
Color 7 (light grey) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB229,229,229\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-light\-grey=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-dark-grey\fR
Color 8 (dark grey) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB127,127,127\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-dark\-grey=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-light-red\fR
Color 9 (light red) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB255,0,0\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-light\-red=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-light-green\fR
Color 10 (light green) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB0,255,0\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-light\-green=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-light-yellow\fR
Color 11 (light yellow) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB255,255,0\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-light\-yellow=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-light-blue\fR
Color 12 (light blue) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB92,92,255\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-light\-blue=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-light-magenta\fR
Color 13 (light magenta) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB255,0,255\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-light\-magenta=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-light-cyan\fR
Color 14 (light cyan) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB0,255,255\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-light\-cyan=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-white\fR
Color 15 (white) when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB255,255,255\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-white=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-foreground\fR
The default text color when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB229,229,229\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-foreground=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpalette-background\fR
The default background color when \fBpalette = custom\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB0,0,0\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-palette\-background=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBunderline-color\fR
The color of underlines (SGR 4 in every style, and SGR 21).
.IP
\fBfg\fR uses the text's color, a palette number uses that palette color, and \fB#rrggbb\fR uses that color. A color a program sets with SGR 58 wins, unless \fBforce\-decoration\-colors\fR is on.
.IP
A program can change a palette color with OSC 4.
.IP
It takes a color: \fBfg\fR, a palette number or \fB#rrggbb\fR: a palette number from 0 to 255, or six hex digits. Default: \fBfg\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-underline\-color=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBstrike-color\fR
The color of strikethrough (SGR 9). It takes the same values as \fBunderline\-color\fR.
.IP
It takes a color: \fBfg\fR, a palette number or \fB#rrggbb\fR: a palette number from 0 to 255, or six hex digits. Default: \fBfg\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-strike\-color=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBforce-decoration-colors\fR
Use \fBunderline\-color\fR even when a program sets its own underline color (SGR 58).
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: off.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-force\-decoration\-colors\fR and \fB\-\-no\-force\-decoration\-colors\fR.
.SS "Keyboard and mouse"
The keyboard layout and key repeat, the mouse and its pointer, and copy and paste.
.TP
\fBxkb-model\fR
The keyboard model, as xkeyboard-config names it.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-xkb\-model=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBxkb-layout\fR
The keyboard layout, such as \fBus\fR or \fBde\fR.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-xkb\-layout=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBxkb-variant\fR
The keyboard layout's variant, such as \fBdvorak\fR.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-xkb\-variant=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBxkb-options\fR
Keyboard options for xkb, separated by commas, such as \fBctrl:nocaps\fR.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-xkb\-options=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBxkb-keymap\fR
A keymap file. When set, vtium uses it instead of the model, layout, variant and options, and falls back to them if the file cannot be used.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-xkb\-keymap=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBxkb-compose-file\fR
A compose table file, used instead of the locale's.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-xkb\-compose\-file=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBxkb-repeat-delay\fR
How long a held key waits before it starts to repeat. 0 means 250 ms.
.IP
It takes a number in ms: 0 to 999; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 250.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-xkb\-repeat\-delay=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBxkb-repeat-rate\fR
The time between the repeats of a held key. 0 means 50 ms.
.IP
It takes a number in ms: 0 to 999; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 50.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-xkb\-repeat\-rate=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBmouse\fR
Use mice, touchpads and touchscreens.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-mouse\fR and \fB\-\-no\-mouse\fR.
.TP
\fBnatural-scrolling\fR
Reverse the mouse wheel.
.IP
The wheel then scrolls the other way, both in vtium's scrollback and for programs.
.IP
This includes the arrow keys vtium sends in full-screen programs (mode 1007). Ctrl with the wheel turned up still zooms in.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: off.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-natural\-scrolling\fR and \fB\-\-no\-natural\-scrolling\fR.
.TP
\fBsoft-cursor\fR
Draw the mouse pointer in software on every display, never with the display's hardware cursor.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: off.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-soft\-cursor\fR and \fB\-\-no\-soft\-cursor\fR.
.TP
\fBpointer-char\fR
Show the mouse pointer as this character, drawn with the console's font.
.IP
When unset, or when the font cannot display the character, the pointer is an arrow.
.IP
The character fills the character cell under the pointer, white with a dark outline, like the arrow.
.IP
It takes one character: no control character or space. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-pointer\-char=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpointer-theme\fR
The SVG file or cursor theme folder whose pictures the pointer takes.
.IP
The value is an SVG file in vtium's pointer file format, or the folder of a desktop cursor theme, such as \fB/usr/share/icons/Adwaita\fR. vtium installs its own pictures as \fB/usr/share/vtium/pointers.svg\fR, which you can copy and edit. The format is described in the pointer file reference <https://vtium.github.io/vtium/reference/pointer-file/>.
.IP
When unset, vtium draws its own pictures. A shape the file lacks keeps vtium's own picture, and \fBpointer\-char\fR keeps the default shape. The font helper draws the pictures in its sandbox, so the console never reads a pointer file's contents. vtium searches no icon folder and follows no \fBInherits=\fR line, so name the folder that holds the \fBcursors\fR folder.
.IP
It takes an absolute path: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-pointer\-theme=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpointer-size\fR
The size of a cursor theme's pointer.
.IP
It is the size a desktop's cursor size setting names. When unset, vtium picks the size nearest the height of a character cell. An SVG pointer file ignores this key, because vtium draws it a cell tall.
.IP
It takes a number in pixels: 8 to 64; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-pointer\-size=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpointer-hide-timeout\fR
Hide the mouse pointer after this long without mouse activity, until it moves again. 0 never hides it.
.IP
It takes a number in s: 0 to 86400; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 20.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-pointer\-hide\-timeout=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBpointer-hide-while-typing\fR
Hide the mouse pointer while you type, until it moves again. A key vtium handles itself, such as a shortcut, does not hide it.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: off.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-pointer\-hide\-while\-typing\fR and \fB\-\-no\-pointer\-hide\-while\-typing\fR.
.TP
\fBclipboard-write\fR
Let programs copy text to vtium's clipboard (OSC 52). When off, vtium ignores what they send.
.IP
vtium keeps the last text copied, by a program or by you, and the middle mouse button and the \fBkey\-paste\fR key paste it.
.IP
The console has no system clipboard.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-clipboard\-write\fR and \fB\-\-no\-clipboard\-write\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-paste\fR
The key that pastes the copied text, as the middle mouse button does; it pastes once per press.
.IP
With Shift, write the character Shift makes: \fB<Ctrl><Shift>V\fR, not \fB<Ctrl><Shift>v\fR. When empty, no key pastes.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Shift>Insert\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-paste=VALUE\fR.
.SS "Key bindings"
The keys vtium acts on itself instead of sending them to the program. Each value lists key combinations, separated by commas, and an empty value hands the key to the program. The scroll keys and the prompt jumps are vtium's only on the main screen: on the alternate screen, where editors run, they reach the program. Ctrl+Alt+F1 to F12 always switch the VT.
.TP
\fBkey-scroll-up\fR
Scroll the view back one line.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Shift>Up\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-scroll\-up=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-scroll-down\fR
Scroll the view forward one line.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Shift>Down\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-scroll\-down=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-page-up\fR
Scroll the view back one page. \fBPrior\fR is Page Up.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Shift>Prior\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-page\-up=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-page-down\fR
Scroll the view forward one page. \fBNext\fR is Page Down.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Shift>Next\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-page\-down=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-prompt-up\fR
Scroll the view back to the shell prompt above, which OSC 133 marks.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl><Shift>Up\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-prompt\-up=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-prompt-down\fR
Scroll the view forward to the shell prompt below, or to the live screen.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl><Shift>Down\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-prompt\-down=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-link-hints\fR
Label each link on the screen with letters, and copy the address of the link whose label you type.
.IP
When empty, no key labels the links.
.IP
Paste the address with the middle button or Shift+Insert. Escape, or this key again, stops without a copy. Only links that a program marks with OSC 8 get a label, on the main screen and on the alternate screen.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl><Shift>O\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-link\-hints=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-zoom-in\fR
Make the font of the console on screen larger.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl>plus,<Ctrl>equal\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-zoom\-in=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-zoom-out\fR
Make the font of the console on screen smaller.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl>minus\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-zoom\-out=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-rotate-cw\fR
Turn the head's displays a quarter turn clockwise. \fBLogo\fR is Super.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Logo>plus,<Logo>equal\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-rotate\-cw=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-rotate-ccw\fR
Turn the head's displays a quarter turn counterclockwise.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Logo>minus\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-rotate\-ccw=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-reset\fR
Reset the console on screen, as the \fBreset\fR command does.
.IP
It clears the screen and the scrollback, and turns off every mode a program set, the keyboard's too: use it when a program left the keyboard sending escape codes.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl><Shift>Delete\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-reset=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-console-next\fR
Show the head's next console.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl><Logo>Right\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-console\-next=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-console-previous\fR
Show the head's previous console.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl><Logo>Left\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-console\-previous=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-console-open\fR
Open a console after the one on screen, unless the policy is \fBclosed\fR.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl><Logo>Return\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-console\-open=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-console-close\fR
Close the console on screen, unless it is the head's last or the policy is \fBclosed\fR.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl><Logo>BackSpace\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-console\-close=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-focus-next\fR
Make the next head active, from a keyboard that roams among the heads.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl><Logo>Up\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-focus\-next=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-focus-previous\fR
Make the previous head active, from a keyboard that roams among the heads.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: \fB<Ctrl><Logo>Down\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-focus\-previous=VALUE\fR.
.SS "Session and terminal"
The command each console runs, the terminal type it sees, and how much of its output vtium keeps.
.TP
\fBlogin\fR
The command each console runs, such as a login prompt or a shell.
.IP
When unset, the command is \fB/bin/login \-p\fR with \fBreset\-env\fR on and \fB/bin/login\fR with it off. A word written \fB$NAME\fR is replaced by that environment variable's value, and \fB$$\fR writes a literal \fB$\fR at the start of a word.
.IP
It takes a command and its arguments: at most 4095 bytes in a file. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[head NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-login \-\- COMMAND...\fR.
.TP
\fBoneshot\fR
Exit when the console's command exits, instead of starting it again.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: off.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-oneshot\fR and \fB\-\-no\-oneshot\fR.
.TP
\fBterm\fR
The value of \fBTERM\fR that programs on the console see.
.IP
The default is vtium's own terminfo entry, \fBxterm\-vtium\fR, which is also named \fBvtium\fR. With either name, vtium answers capability queries (XTGETTCAP) from that entry. With \fBkmscon\fR, vtium answers from kmscon's entry. With any other value, vtium answers every query as unknown.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: \fBxterm\-vtium\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-term=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBreset-env\fR
Start each console's command with only the environment variables vtium sets. When off, the command also gets vtium's own environment.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-reset\-env\fR and \fB\-\-no\-reset\-env\fR.
.TP
\fBbackspace-delete\fR
Backspace sends DEL (0x7f). When off, it sends BS (0x08).
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-backspace\-delete\fR and \fB\-\-no\-backspace\-delete\fR.
.TP
\fBsb-size\fR
How many lines of scrollback each console keeps, up to \fBsb\-size\-cap\fR.
.IP
It takes a number in lines: 0 to 100000; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 1000.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-sb\-size=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBsb-size-cap\fR
The most lines \fBsb\-size\fR can ask for.
.IP
When unset, it depends on the machine's memory: 10,000 lines below 2 GiB, 30,000 from 2 GiB and 100,000 from 4 GiB.
.IP
It takes a number in lines: 0 to 100000; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-sb\-size\-cap=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBsb-memory\fR
The most memory each console's scrollback uses. When it is full, vtium drops the oldest lines, as it does past \fBsb\-size\fR.
.IP
The value is in bytes, or in KiB, MiB or GiB with \fBK\fR, \fBM\fR or \fBG\fR, such as \fB6M\fR. It can be up to a quarter of the machine's memory. When vtium cannot read \fB/proc/meminfo\fR, it can be up to the default.
.IP
When unset, vtium sizes it to fit each console's size in characters, and sizes it again when that changes.
.IP
A console of up to 170\[u00D7]48 characters gets what is left of a 16 MiB budget, after the console's screens and the rest of vtium. So a smaller console keeps more lines. A larger console gets the 170\[u00D7]48 value shown here.
.IP
The default does not grow with the display or the machine's memory. On a machine with memory to spare, set \fBsb\-memory\fR for a deeper scrollback. A value that you set stays fixed.
.IP
It takes a number in bytes: 1048576 to 1073741824; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 5373952 without image support, 4915200 with it.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-sb\-memory=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBbell\fR
Ring the console's bell when a program sends BEL.
.IP
A program can silence its own bells by resetting mode 1042, which starts set. When off, no bell rings.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: off.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-bell\fR and \fB\-\-no\-bell\fR.
.TP
\fBsync-timeout\fR
The longest vtium waits for a program to finish a synchronized update (mode 2026) before it shows the screen as it is. 0 turns mode 2026 off.
.IP
It takes a number in ms: 0 to 10000; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 200.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-sync\-timeout=VALUE\fR.
.SS "Consoles and the VT"
How many consoles the heads can have, and the VT vtium runs on.
.TP
\fBhead-consoles-max\fR
The most consoles one head can have. A head's \fBconsoles\fR cannot open more than this.
.IP
It takes a number in consoles: 1 to 50; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 4.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-head\-consoles\-max=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBconsoles-max\fR
The most consoles all heads can have together.
.IP
Every head keeps its first console. More consoles open, in the order the heads appear in vtium.conf, until the total reaches this number.
.IP
It takes a number in consoles: 1 to 50; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 8.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-consoles\-max=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBvt\fR
The VT to run on: a number N for \fB/dev/ttyN\fR, a name under \fB/dev\fR, or a path. When unset, vtium runs on the VT it was started on, or else the next free one.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-vt=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBswitchvt\fR
Switch to vtium's VT when vtium starts. When off, vtium waits until something else switches to it.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-switchvt\fR and \fB\-\-no\-switchvt\fR.
.SS "On\-screen display and notices"
What vtium shows on top of the console, and in which colors. It shows which head and console you are looking at, a notice about a problem, and the kernel's warnings.
.TP
\fBosd\fR
What the on-screen display shows at the top of each screen after a change, such as a console switch.
.IP
\fBfull\fR shows a line across the top, such as \fB\[u25CF] desk \[u00B7] console 2 of 3\fR. \fBminimal\fR shows a dot in the top-right character cell: filled on the active head, hollow on the others. \fBoff\fR shows nothing. Under these three, a notice about a problem still appears, such as one about errors in vtium.conf.
.IP
\fBsilent\fR shows nothing, and it hides vtium's notices too. vtium still writes each notice to its log. Two screens appear even under \fBsilent\fR, because no console runs while they show. One is the panic screen of a closed vtium.conf that cannot run. The other is the notice that vtium stopped every screen because a session kept ending.
.IP
It takes one of \fBfull\fR, \fBminimal\fR, \fBoff\fR, \fBsilent\fR. Default: \fBminimal\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-osd=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBosd-timeout\fR
How long the on-screen display stays visible after a change. \fBnever\fR keeps it visible all the time.
.IP
It takes a number in s, or \fBnever\fR: 1 to 600; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 3.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-osd\-timeout=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBfocus-mark\fR
The old name for \fBosd\fR and \fBosd\-timeout\fR.
.IP
vtium reads it only when \fBosd\fR and \fBosd\-timeout\fR are not set: \fBauto\fR means \fBosd = minimal\fR, \fBalways\fR means \fBosd = minimal\fR with \fBosd\-timeout = never\fR, and \fBoff\fR means \fBosd = off\fR. When it is set, vtium logs the new keys.
.IP
It takes one of \fBauto\fR, \fBalways\fR, \fBoff\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-focus\-mark=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkernel-messages\fR
When vtium shows the kernel's warnings and errors on the screen.
.IP
vtium shows each new kernel message whose level is warning or more severe, on top of the console, as long as the kernel's console log level lets it through. \fBquiet\fR and \fBloglevel=\fR on the kernel command line set that level. vtium never writes them into a console's session.
.IP
\fBafter\-plymouth\fR leaves the messages to Plymouth, the boot splash program, while it runs, and vtium shows them once Plymouth has quit. Once Plymouth has quit, vtium does not go back and show the messages that came while it ran. On a machine without Plymouth, vtium shows them from its start. \fBalways\fR shows them whether Plymouth runs or not.
.IP
It takes one of \fBafter\-plymouth\fR, \fBalways\fR. Default: \fBafter\-plymouth\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-kernel\-messages=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBnotice-foreground\fR
The text color of a notice. A notice is a box that reports a problem, such as errors in vtium.conf. It appears at every level of \fBosd\fR except \fBsilent\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB31,41,51\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-notice\-foreground=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBnotice-background\fR
The background color of a notice. While a notice is showing, the on-screen display's line appears in the same box, in the notice's colors.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB254,226,226\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-notice\-background=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBnotice-border\fR
The border color of a notice.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB185,28,28\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-notice\-border=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBosd-foreground\fR
The text color of the on-screen display: the line of \fBosd = full\fR and the dot of \fBosd = minimal\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB229,231,235\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-osd\-foreground=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBosd-background\fR
The background color of the on-screen display: behind the line of \fBosd = full\fR, and in the character cell behind the dot of \fBosd = minimal\fR.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB31,41,51\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-osd\-background=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBosd-border\fR
The border color of the on-screen display with \fBosd = full\fR. The dot of \fBosd = minimal\fR has no border.
.IP
It takes a color, \fBr,g,b\fR: each part 0 to 255. Default: \fB97,110,124\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-osd\-border=VALUE\fR.
.SS Displays
Each display's resolution, colors and density, how long vtium waits for a display when it starts, and how it draws to them.
.TP
\fBuse-original-mode\fR
Keep each display's current resolution. When off, vtium sets each display's preferred resolution.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: off.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-use\-original\-mode\fR and \fB\-\-no\-use\-original\-mode\fR.
.TP
\fBmode\fR
A display's resolution and refresh rate.
.IP
\fBWxH[@RATE]\fR sets it for every display, \fB[CONNECTOR]WxH\fR for one connector, and \fBGPU[CONNECTOR]WxH\fR for one connector on one graphics card, named by its udev \fBID_PATH\fR. Write one \fBmode\fR line per display; the most specific match wins. When unset, each display uses its preferred resolution. Setting it turns \fBuse\-original\-mode\fR off.
.IP
It takes a display mode, one to a line: a name of at most 63 bytes, and each side 1 to 65535. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-mode=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBdisplay-depth\fR
How many colors or shades each display can show.
.IP
\fB1\fR is black and white: each character cell is lit or dark by its colors, never by their brightness. \fBgray4\fR and \fBgray16\fR are for gray panels of 4 or 16 shades; vtium sends color and the panel converts it. \fBcolor\fR is full color. \fBauto\fR uses what the display reports.
.IP
For \fBauto\fR, an fbdev device that reports 1 bit gets \fB1\fR, and a known DRM panel driver gives its levels.
.IP
It takes one of \fBauto\fR, \fB1\fR, \fBgray4\fR, \fBgray16\fR, \fBcolor\fR. Default: \fBauto\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-display\-depth=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBdisplay-dpi\fR
The pixel density (DPI) used to turn \fBfont\-size\fR and a font set's \fBsize=\fR from points into pixels, instead of the density each display reports.
.IP
When unset, vtium measures each display's density from the physical size it reports. If a display reports none, vtium assumes 96 DPI and logs a warning once for that display.
.IP
It takes a number in dpi: 20 to 1000; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-display\-dpi=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBdisplay-wait\fR
How long vtium waits for a display when it starts, counted from when its VT is shown.
.IP
If no display appears in that time, vtium exits with status 1, so the unit's \fBOnFailure=\fR can start a getty. When vtium.conf's policy is closed, vtium waits as long as it takes, as with \fBforever\fR.
.IP
vtium counts only while the VT is its own. If another program takes the VT before vtium's first frame is on screen, vtium waits for the VT to come back and then starts the full wait again.
.IP
X does this at boot when it starts on another VT.
.IP
With 0, vtium does not wait: only a display that is ready when the VT is shown counts. \fBforever\fR waits as long as it takes.
.IP
\fBforever\fR suits a machine whose only display is a USB adapter (DisplayLink, \fBudl\fR) that may be plugged in at any time.
.IP
Once a display has appeared, vtium never exits for lack of one.
.IP
It takes a number in s, or \fBforever\fR: 0 to 600; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 10.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-display\-wait=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBdisplay-buffers\fR
How many frame buffers each DRM display keeps, the same number for every display.
.IP
\fB3\fR is triple buffering. No frame waits for the previous one to reach the screen, and each display needs one more frame of memory. \fB2\fR is double buffering. \fB1\fR is single buffering, which uses the least memory. vtium then draws into the buffer on screen, so a half-drawn frame can show.
.IP
A framebuffer device (fbdev) always keeps one buffer. When the key is unset, the build decides. A build uses the number it was built with, \fBVTIUM_DISPLAY_BUFFERS\fR. Without one, it uses 3 in a build with optional features, 2 in the smallest build (minspec), and 1 only in a build smaller than that.
.IP
\fBvtium config get display\-buffers\fR prints the draft's value followed by \fBdraft\fR. When the draft sets none, it prints the build's default followed by \fBdefault\fR. That default is \fBunset\fR when the build sets no number, and the rule above then decides.
.IP
It takes one of \fB1\fR, \fB2\fR, \fB3\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-display\-buffers=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBhwaccel\fR
Draw with the GPU, in a build that supports it.
.IP
It is off unless vtium was built with it on (\fBVTIUM_HWACCEL\fR).
.IP
\fBvtium config get hwaccel\fR prints the draft's value followed by \fBdraft\fR, or the build's default followed by \fBdefault\fR. \fBvtium config set hwaccel on\fR turns it on in the draft.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: off.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-hwaccel\fR and \fB\-\-no\-hwaccel\fR.
.TP
\fBvtlook\fR
The vtlook, a file of frame filters that change the picture before the display shows it, such as a CRT effect.
.IP
The value names \fBNAME.vtlook\fR in \fB/etc/vtium/looks\fR or a preset such as \fBamber\fR. A look's own \fBvtlook\fR wins over this one. Unset or \fBdefault\fR is the built-in vtlook, which changes no pixel.
.IP
vtium looks for the file in \fB/etc/vtium/looks\fR and then in \fB/usr/share/vtium/looks\fR, where the package installs its presets, so a file in \fB/etc/vtium/looks\fR takes the place of a preset of the same name. When the vtlook has an error, the head draws the built-in vtlook, and vtium logs where the vtlook failed. A build without frame filters accepts this key, logs one line, and draws the built-in vtlook.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-vtlook=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBrender-timer\fR
Measure each display's frame times once a second, and write the figures to the log, show them on screen, or both.
.IP
With \fBrender\-timer = osd\fR, vtium shows the figures at the top of each display, in the on-screen display's box. When \fBosd = silent\fR, vtium shows no figures on screen: \fBrender\-timer = osd\fR then shows nothing, and \fBrender\-timer = both\fR writes only the log.
.IP
It takes one of \fBnone\fR, \fBosd\fR, \fBlog\fR, \fBboth\fR. Default: \fBnone\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-render\-timer=VALUE\fR.
.SS "Power and blanking"
When the displays turn off, and the keys that reboot, power off or suspend the machine.
.TP
\fBdpms-timeout\fR
Turn the displays off after this long with no key press or mouse action (a move, a click or the wheel). 0 never turns them off.
.IP
It takes a number in s: 0 to 4294967295; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 600.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-dpms\-timeout=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBprogram-blank\fR
Let a program set the blanking timeout and wake the displays, as \fBsetterm \-\-blank\fR does on the Linux console.
.IP
A program sets the timeout with \fBCSI 9 ; n ]\fR (n minutes, up to 60; 0 never blanks) and wakes the displays with \fBCSI 13 ]\fR. A terminal reset (RIS) returns the timeout to \fBdpms\-timeout\fR. When off, vtium ignores these sequences.
.IP
A key press or mouse action wakes the displays either way.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-program\-blank\fR and \fB\-\-no\-program\-blank\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-reboot\fR
The key that reboots the machine.
.IP
When unset, no key reboots.
.IP
When \fB/proc/sys/kernel/ctrl\-alt\-del\fR is 0, vtium asks init to reboot, as Ctrl+Alt+Del does, so the services stop first; otherwise the kernel restarts at once.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-reboot=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-power-off\fR
The key that powers the machine off.
.IP
When unset, no key powers off.
.IP
vtium asks init with \fBSIGRTMIN+4\fR, which systemd treats as \fBpoweroff.target\fR, so the services stop and the filesystems are unmounted first.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-power\-off=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBkey-suspend\fR
The key that suspends the machine to RAM.
.IP
vtium asks logind to suspend. When logind is not running, or vtium was built without \fBlogind\fR, vtium writes \fBmem\fR to \fB/sys/power/state\fR. When unset, no key suspends.
.IP
Because vtium asks logind, anything that holds off sleep through logind can still do so.
.IP
It takes key combinations, such as \fB<Ctrl><Alt>Delete\fR, separated by commas: modifiers Shift, Lock, Control, Alt and Logo, and keys as xkb names them. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-key\-suspend=VALUE\fR.
.SS Images
Sixel and kitty graphics, how much memory images use, which protocols vtium offers, and the helper that reads and decodes them.
.TP
\fBimage-storage-limit\fR
The most memory each console uses for images, on both its screens together.
.IP
The value is in bytes, or in KiB, MiB or GiB with \fBK\fR, \fBM\fR or \fBG\fR (such as \fB64M\fR), up to 1 TiB. \fBauto\fR gives each console enough for four screenfuls of its head's displays, from 16 MiB to 192 MiB. \fB0\fR turns sixel and kitty graphics off.
.IP
\fBauto\fR recalculates when a display is added, removed or resized.
.IP
A build without image support accepts this key and shows no images.
.IP
It takes a number in bytes, or \fBauto\fR: 0 to 1099511627776; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: \fBauto\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-image\-storage\-limit=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBsixel\fR
Show sixel images.
.IP
When off, vtium neither offers nor shows sixel: its device attributes (DA1) leave out attribute 4, and it ignores sixel data. vtium offers sixel only while \fBimage\-storage\-limit\fR is above 0 and every display of the console's head can show images. A build without image support never offers it.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-sixel\fR and \fB\-\-no\-sixel\fR.
.TP
\fBkitty-graphics\fR
Show images sent with the kitty graphics protocol.
.IP
When off, vtium neither offers nor shows them: it ignores graphics commands and does not answer queries. vtium offers it only while \fBimage\-storage\-limit\fR is above 0 and every display of the console's head can show images. A build without image support never offers it.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-kitty\-graphics\fR and \fB\-\-no\-kitty\-graphics\fR.
.TP
\fBmax-graphic-size\fR
The largest image a program can send, in pixels (\fBWxH\fR), for sixel and kitty graphics alike. vtium refuses a larger one.
.IP
A build without image support accepts this key and shows no images.
.IP
It takes a size, \fBWxH\fR: each side 1 to 65535. Default: \fB10000x10000\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-max\-graphic\-size=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBnum-color-registers\fR
How many colors a sixel image can use (its color registers), and the most a program can ask for with XTSMGRAPHICS.
.IP
A build without image support accepts this key and shows no images.
.IP
It takes a number in registers: 2 to 65535; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 1024.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-num\-color\-registers=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBprivate-color-registers\fR
Give each sixel image its own colors (mode 1070, after a reset).
.IP
When on, each sixel image starts from the default colors. When off, images share their colors. A build without image support accepts this key and shows no images.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: on.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-private\-color\-registers\fR and \fB\-\-no\-private\-color\-registers\fR.
.TP
\fBimage-helper\fR
The image helper: the program that vtium starts, as the logged-in user, to read and decode images. It reads image files and shared memory for kitty graphics, and decodes PNG and zlib-compressed images. vtium runs as root and does neither itself.
.IP
vtium opens the program once, when it starts, and runs every helper from that open file. So a newly installed helper takes effect when vtium restarts.
.IP
vtium refuses a helper whose path is not absolute, or that is not a regular file. It refuses a helper that is setuid or setgid. It also refuses a helper that anyone but root or vtium's own account owns, or that its group or other users can write. It then logs why, and refuses images. It answers \fBEBADF\fR for files and shared memory, and \fBEINVAL\fR for PNG and zlib. vtium starts the helper through \fB/proc/self/fd\fR, so \fB/proc\fR must be mounted.
.IP
A build without image support accepts this key and starts no helper.
.IP
The default is the path vtium was built with.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: \fB/usr/bin/vtium\-image\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-image\-helper=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBlogin-image\fR
A PNG or JPEG picture shown at each login prompt.
.IP
\fBlogin\-image\-fit\fR chooses whether it is a background beneath the text or a banner above or below the prompt. The picture is an ordinary picture on the screen: it scrolls away with the text, and a screen clear removes it. A program can cover or remove the picture, but it can never read it.
.IP
To remove the picture each time you log in, add one line to your shell's startup file. See Remove the login picture when you log in <https://vtium.github.io/vtium/configure/#remove-the-login-picture-when-you-log-in>.
.IP
vtium reads the file as root and decodes it in the image helper, as the account that runs before login. When vtium cannot read or decode the file, it logs one line and starts the login prompt without the picture. When unset, vtium shows no picture.
.IP
A head shows the picture of the look it names. A look that sets no picture takes the top level's. A build without image support accepts this key, logs one line, and shows no picture.
.IP
It takes an absolute path: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-login\-image=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBlogin-image-fit\fR
\fBcontain\fR, \fBcover\fR, \fBstretch\fR, \fBnone\fR or \fBbanner\fR.
.IP
\fBcontain\fR, the default, scales the background until it fits inside the display, keeping its shape, and the default background shows in the bands left over. \fBcover\fR scales it until it covers the display, keeping its shape, and cuts off what passes the edges. \fBstretch\fR fills the display exactly. \fBnone\fR shows the background at its own size. \fBlogin\-image\-scale\fR can then shrink the background, and \fBlogin\-image\-position\fR places it.
.IP
\fBbanner\fR shows the picture like a message of the day. It sits at the top of the screen with the login prompt below it, or at the bottom with the prompt above it when \fBlogin\-image\-position\fR names the bottom.
.IP
A background shows through every character cell whose background is the default color. Text, colored cells and every picture a program sends cover it.
.IP
It takes one of \fBcontain\fR, \fBcover\fR, \fBstretch\fR, \fBnone\fR, \fBbanner\fR. Default: \fBcontain\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-login\-image\-fit=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBlogin-image-rows\fR
A banner's height in text rows.
.IP
vtium scales the banner to that height, keeping its shape. When unset, the banner shows at its own size in pixels. A banner is never taller than the screen's rows less two, so the login prompt always has room. A background ignores this key.
.IP
It takes a number in rows: 1 to 1000; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-login\-image\-rows=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBlogin-image-position\fR
Where the picture sits: \fBtop\-left\fR to \fBbottom\-right\fR.
.IP
vtium places a background against the edges of the text grid that the anchor names, and centers it along a side that the anchor leaves out. For example, \fBtop\fR puts the picture against the top of the grid, centered across the screen. When unset, a background is centered.
.IP
A banner sits at the top of the screen, or at the bottom under \fBbottom\-left\fR, \fBbottom\fR or \fBbottom\-right\fR. The anchor's column places it across the screen, and when unset the banner sits at the top left. A centered banner with an odd number of spare columns leaves the extra column on its right.
.IP
vtium starts the login prompt on the first row above a banner at the bottom. Text that reaches the banner prints over it, and line feeds then scroll it away.
.IP
It takes one of \fBtop\-left\fR, \fBtop\fR, \fBtop\-right\fR, \fBleft\fR, \fBcenter\fR, \fBright\fR, \fBbottom\-left\fR, \fBbottom\fR, \fBbottom\-right\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-login\-image\-position=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBlogin-image-scale\fR
A background's size, in percent of its fit's size.
.IP
The value is a whole number, written without a \fB%\fR sign. At 30, a contained picture fits inside 30% of the display's width and height. The default, 100, keeps the size the fit gives, so no value makes a picture larger. A banner ignores this key, since \fBlogin\-image\-rows\fR sizes a banner.
.IP
It takes a number in %: 1 to 100; vtium clamps a number outside that range. Default: 100.
.IP
Set it in the top level or a \fB[look NAME]\fR section, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-login\-image\-scale=VALUE\fR.
.SS "Running vtium"
Where vtium finds its configuration, its log, the font helper that draws a font's characters, and the character cache.
.TP
\fBdebug\fR
Log debug messages too, to help find what went wrong. When off, vtium logs information messages, warnings and errors.
.IP
It takes a switch, on or off. Default: off.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-debug\fR and \fB\-\-no\-debug\fR.
.TP
\fBfont-helper\fR
The font helper: the program vtium starts to draw a font set's characters. It runs as \fBnobody\fR when vtium runs as root.
.IP
vtium opens the program once, when it starts, and runs every helper from that open file. So a newly installed helper takes effect when vtium restarts.
.IP
vtium refuses a helper whose path is not absolute, or that is not a regular file. It refuses a helper that is setuid or setgid. It also refuses a helper that anyone but root or vtium's own account owns, or that its group or other users can write. It then logs why and uses its built-in font. vtium starts the helper through \fB/proc/self/fd\fR, so \fB/proc\fR must be mounted.
.IP
The default is the path vtium was built with.
.IP
It takes text: at most 4095 bytes, with no control character. Default: \fB/usr/bin/vtium\-config\fR.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-font\-helper=VALUE\fR.
.TP
\fBglyph-cache\fR
How much memory vtium uses to cache drawn characters. When unset, \fBminspec\fR (small), or \fBperformance\fR in a build with the \fBperformance\-cache\fR feature.
.IP
It takes one of \fBminspec\fR, \fBperformance\fR. Default: unset.
.IP
Set it in the top level, or on vtium's command line with \fB\-\-glyph\-cache=VALUE\fR.
.SH SECTIONS
A look is a font and its size. A head is a screen: one or more displays that show the same consoles, with the input devices that type into them. The rules send each display and each input device to a head. The policy decides what happens to a device that no rule names.
.PP
vtium.conf's sections: its looks, its heads, and the rules that send each display and each input device to a head.
.PP
The settings come first, above the first section heading: vtium's own keys, which every head uses unless it sets its own, and the two keys of the sections, version and policy, in any order. When vtium.conf has no section, vtium uses the sections below, built into it from this same text, so a file written from them changes nothing until you edit it.
.PP
vtium reads vtium.conf only when root owns it and neither its group nor other users can write it or a directory above it. vtium never changes the file. It logs each error with its line, and ignores what the error takes: a bad rule ignores that rule, an error in a head's section ignores that head and every rule that names it, an error in a look's section ignores that look, and an error in the structure of the sections, such as a missing version, ignores everything but the policy. An error here never makes vtium ignore a setting above the first section. vtium check-config FILE checks a file before you install it.
.PP
A file with a section needs version = 1. A # starts a comment at the start of a line or after a blank; a # inside a word, as in \-\-x#1, is part of the word.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
version = 1
.fi
.RE
.PP
The policy decides what the rules leave unsaid. With open, an input device no rule places roams, typing into the active head; no head is isolated unless it says so; and the keyboard safeguards, anti-lockout and the rescue key, are on. With closed, an input device no rule places is ignored; every head is isolated unless it says otherwise; and the keyboard safeguards are off, so no keyboard reaches a head the file does not send it to. With either policy, when the rules leave every display off, vtium turns on the boot display: with open for the first head, with closed only to show a notice.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# policy = open
.fi
.RE
.PP
A look is a font and its size, which a head uses. A [look NAME] section takes font-name, which names its font set (frit); the font settings above; vtlook; and cell, the character cell in pixels, written as a font set's cell= line writes it. In one look, font-size overrides cell. The built-in configuration has no look, so its head uses the settings above. For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[look big]
font\-name = desktop
font\-size = 14
.fi
.RE
.PP
A head is a screen: one or more displays that show the same consoles, with the input devices sent to it, a look and its consoles. A name has 1 to 64 of A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ + \-, and roaming, ignore and off cannot be used. Each key below is shown at its default.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[head console]
.fi
.RE
.PP
The look the head uses, the name of a [look NAME] section. When unset, the look is default: the [look default] section where there is one, else the settings above the first section.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# look = default
.fi
.RE
.PP
How many consoles the head has, 1 to 50; it shows one at a time.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# consoles = 1
.fi
.RE
.PP
The command the head's consoles run, written as vtium.conf's login takes it. When unset, the consoles run vtium.conf's login.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# login = /bin/login \-p
.fi
.RE
.PP
What the head's sessions do when it loses its last display: keep runs them on until a display of the head returns; hangup hangs them up at once; hangup DURATION (30, 30s, 5m or 1h) keeps them if a display returns within that time and hangs them up after it.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# when\-display\-lost = keep
.fi
.RE
.PP
yes isolates the head: Ctrl-Alt-F<n> on its keyboards reaches its session as keys and switches no VT, the rescue key leaves its keyboards where they are, and it is never the active head, so no roaming input device reaches it. When unset, it is no with policy = open and yes with policy = closed.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# isolated = no
.fi
.RE
.PP
yes lets the head's own keyboards reach it from another VT. On a VT where the head does not run, its displays show a notice that names its VT, and Enter on a keyboard the rules send to the head switches to the first of the head's VTs on which vtium runs. Other keys only wake the notice, but Ctrl-Alt and a function key still switch the VT. It works only with
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# policy = open, and never on an isolated head.
# switch\-on\-enter = no
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[rules]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each display and each input device goes through these rules on its own, top to bottom, and the first rule that matches it sends it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
display MATCH \-> TARGET
input MATCH \-> TARGET
.fi
.RE
.PP
MATCH is * (every device of that kind) or one or more key:value matches, all of which must hold. TARGET is a head's name; for an input device, roaming (it types into the active head) or ignore (vtium never opens it, except that with the open policy it watches a keyboard for the rescue key); for a display, off (vtium turns it off). A display no rule matches stays off, and vtium logs a rule line to paste for it; an input device no rule matches roams with the open policy and is ignored with the closed one. Run \fBvtium config flow \-\-json\fR to list every device with its keys filled in.
.PP
Keys for an input device:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
path:ID_PATH                  the USB port, and every interface below it
serial:VENDOR:PRODUCT:SERIAL  one unit, wherever it is connected
bluetooth:ADDRESS             one device, once bonded
id:VENDOR:PRODUCT             every unit of a model; devices lie
name:GLOB                     any device that gives itself that name
class:CLASS                   keyboard, key, mouse, touchpad,
                              touchscreen, trackball, pointingstick,
                              tablet, tablet\-pad, joystick,
                              accelerometer or switch, as udev tags it
udev:NAME=GLOB                any udev property of the device
.fi
.RE
.PP
For a head whose sessions need protecting, use path plus serial where the device has a serial, and path alone otherwise. id or name alone is a convenience, not a protection. In a glob, * matches any run of characters and ? one character; a value has no blank, so ? stands for a space in a name.
.PP
Keys for a display:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
edid:MAKER:PRODUCT:SERIAL     one monitor, the recommended key
connector\-path:PATH           one port of a DisplayPort multi\-stream hub,
                              globs allowed
connector:CARD/CONNECTOR      a port of a graphics card, globs allowed
card:GLOB                     every output of a graphics card
.fi
.RE
.PP
A card is its udev ID_PATH (pci-0000:03:00.0), never cardN. A multi-stream connector's name, such as DP-8, can change after a replug or a resume; its PATH (mst:...) stays the same, so use connector-path there and connector only where the name is stable. The first number of the PATH, the card connector the hub is connected to, can change after a kernel or firmware update.
.PP
path, serial, card, connector and udev read udev's database, and match nothing on a system that does not run udev.
.PP
To customize, add rules above the built-in ones, such as:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
display edid:AAA:0x0001:LARGE00001 \-> desk
input path:pci\-0000:15:00.0\-usb\-0:3.3 \-> desk
input udev:VTIUM_IGNORE=1 \-> ignore
.fi
.RE
.PP
A udev rule copied into /etc/udev/rules.d/ can mark a device for the last of these (the examples are in /usr/share/doc/vtium/examples/):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{name}=="Headset", ENV{VTIUM_IGNORE}="1"
.fi
.RE
.PP
and an hwdb entry copied into /etc/udev/hwdb.d/ can mark a security token or a numpad, which anti-lockout then does not count as a keyboard:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
evdev:input:b0003vBBBBp0001*
 VTIUM_ROLE=token
.fi
.RE
.PP
The built-in rules: one head showing the same consoles on every display, with every keyboard, key device and pointing device typing into it, and every other input device ignored.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
display * \-> console
input class:keyboard \-> roaming
input class:mouse \-> roaming
input class:touchpad \-> roaming
input class:touchscreen \-> roaming
input class:trackball \-> roaming
input class:pointingstick \-> roaming
input class:key \-> roaming
input * \-> ignore
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB/etc/vtium/vtium.conf\fR
The configuration file.
.TP
\fB/etc/vtium/vtium.conf.example\fR
Every setting at its default, commented out, and the built-in sections.
.TP
\fB/etc/vtium/fonts\fR
The font sets (frits) that \fBfont\-name\fR names, each in a file \fBNAME.vtfrit\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
vtium(8), vtium-config(1)
